WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:16.041
vmctl needs Linux: KVM, /proc, Unix sockets. On Windows 11 it lives in WSL2, a real Linux kernel next to Windows. This is a Windows with nothing on it yet.

00:00:16.141 --> 00:00:26.041
From the catalog site, download the Windows installer.

00:00:26.141 --> 00:00:52.041
Edge warns: it is a script, nobody signed it. Keep it, then open it.

00:00:52.141 --> 00:01:30.041
Windows asks once more. Choose Run: the default button is Cancel.

00:01:30.141 --> 00:02:05.041
First run: it installs WSL. Windows asks for administrator rights, then wants a restart.

00:02:05.141 --> 00:03:03.041
Restart, and run the same installer again.

00:03:03.141 --> 00:04:09.791
Second run: it downloads and installs Ubuntu 24.04.

00:04:09.891 --> 00:04:37.791
At its first start Ubuntu asks for a Linux user: it proposes your Windows name, Enter accepts it. Then a password, twice: sudo will ask for it in a minute.

00:04:37.891 --> 00:05:39.458
Exit, and run it a third time: now it clones the project inside Ubuntu and runs setup.sh, as on any Linux.

00:05:39.558 --> 00:06:35.458
setup.sh lists what it would install and asks first: y, then the Linux password for sudo.

00:06:35.558 --> 00:07:02.791
QEMU, the firmware, the tools. A few minutes, no questions.

00:07:02.891 --> 00:07:24.791
The welcome screen: Enter, the installer has not finished yet.

00:07:24.891 --> 00:07:58.791
Last step: your Linux user joins the kvm group, and QEMU ISO Lab lands on the desktop and in the Start menu.

00:07:58.891 --> 00:08:42.291
From here on, one double-click.

00:08:42.391 --> 00:09:11.291
The first time the dashboard asks for the guest user of your machines.

00:09:11.391 --> 00:09:24.291
And here is the whole catalog, from Windows: the same lab as on Linux.

00:09:24.391 --> 00:09:38.321
Three runs, then an icon. The details are in the README.
